// EXPECT: E0277 — a `noted_name` index that is not an integer literal (ADR-0100 §1)
//
// **`noted_name`'s index must be a literal, and this is the boundary of folding rather than a spelling
// limitation.** The answer is computed while *checking* — which is what lets the whole note query need no VM,
// no table and no new query (ADR-0099 §2) — so every argument must be readable then. A local is not, and a
// `for` variable is emphatically not: it exists only at run time.
//
// So the loop a build script really wants,
//
//     for i: 0..noted_count("serialise") { name := noted_name("serialise", i); … }
//
// cannot be made to work by folding at all. It needs the query to lower to *real code* reading a
// **compiler-emitted table**: static data the back end emits and the VM can also read. Jairs has no such
// mechanism — it is the same one `Type_Info`'s variable-length field list has been deferred for since
// ADR-0078 — and it is owed its own wave. ADR-0100 §2 says so in those words rather than implying the message
// loop is nearly done.
//
// Until then a script unrolls to a fixed bound, which is why an **out-of-range** index answers `""` rather
// than being refused: the tail of an unrolled loop has to be quiet. Only an *unreadable* index is refused,
// and that distinction is the whole design — "I cannot see the index" and "there is nothing at that index"
// are different findings, and only the first is the author's mistake.
//
// No `#import "Basic"`: an unused import is E0231, a second diagnostic this directory's one-per-file contract
// does not allow. The intrinsics are recognised by the compiler rather than imported.

a :: (x: s64) -> s64 @serialise {
    return x;
}

asks_with_a_local :: () {
    i := 0;
    // A local, so the name resolves — the refusal is about *when* its value is readable, not whether it is
    // spelled correctly.
    which := noted_name("serialise", i);
}
